The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says *known, one issue, one fix queued*. Its own table
already disagrees: it counts two causes, and a footnote says some reports were filed under
`parser` although the desk read them as something else. This page splits #4471 by what each
report says, not by its subject line, and gives every problem its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser closes one of these tickets. The other two keep coming in.
</Callout>

## What is in #4471

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} {what} />

Each report is counted once: the ticket export comes in overlapping pages, so some reports
appear twice under the same id. The count above is by unique id.

## How a report was placed

1. **Is it a one-day move?** The desk read these reports as a date that moved by one day,
   not a swapped day and month (`desk-notes.json`). They go to `one-day`, whatever their tenant.
2. **Otherwise, by tenant** (`causes.ts`): a day-first tenant's report that came in through
   a `booking-v3` form is `hint`; every other one is `parser`.

The subject line and intake's tag decide nothing. Intake tags from the reporter's browser, and
it is wrong both ways: some `hint` reports are tagged `parser`, and more `parser` reports are
tagged `hint`.

## The three problems

**`parser`: month-first parser.** "Booked for 2 March. The invite says 3 February." Every day
and month swap that did not come through a `booking-v3` form. One fix: parse dates in the
tenant's locale. This is the fix #4471 has queued.

**`hint`: booking-v3 date hint.** Only `form` reports from tenants on `booking-v3`. The form
tells people to type `MM/DD/YYYY` and then reads the date day first. The parser fix does not
touch it: the form's hint and its reading have to agree.

**`one-day`: one-day shift.** "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May."
"Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting." "Every booking in
June is one day off." No month is swapped, so the parser fix does not touch it either. It looks
like a time zone or DST conversion, and it needs its own investigation.

## What the desk sees today, for comparison

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## For the desk

- Merging by subject put all three problems into one ticket ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
  Subjects such as "Wrong day on the invite" appear in more than one problem.
- Before you mark a big ticket *known*, read its cause table. If it shows more than one row,
  or a footnote, it is more than one ticket.

